our_verified_findings.finding:
Other hidden assumptions: infinite bandwidth, no queueing and no loss;
      one global alpha applied even to exact hits; popularity independent of similarity;
      the client can judge similarity (false for LLM answers); free similarity lookup;
      tandem topology only.

tasks.task:
Evaluate UnetStack, ns-3 and MATLAB for this project. Address:
(a) existing support: ns-3's ndnSIM (Content Store policies, forwarding strategies,
    current version and ns-3 compatibility) and the ns-3 UAN module; UnetStack's
    agent architecture, simulator vs. real-modem modes, and available PHY/MAC/routing;
    MATLAB's role (analysis, Monte Carlo, any toolboxes);
(b) how hard it is to implement similarity lookup in each, and where it would live
    (for example, ndnSIM ContentStore::find, or a UnetStack agent);
(c) the measurements each makes credible (packet-level delay, queueing, loss,
    energy);
(d) the learning curve for a two-person team within one semester;
(e) how each choice changes which problem statements make sense.
Recommend one tool, with reasons, and name the strongest objection to that choice.

Produce 5-8 refined problem statements, each tied to the chosen tool. For each, give:
the one-paragraph statement ("Given ..., design ..., and validate in <tool> ...");
the gap, traced to a specific base-paper equation, an assumption, or a cited paper;
the core mechanism or idea; the analysis or derivation we can do; the experiment
design (topology, parameters, workloads, baselines, metrics); the expected result as
a falsifiable hypothesis; a one-line novelty claim against the closest prior work;
the five hardest questions an MTech/PhD examiner would ask, with answers; and effort,
risk and fallback.
